  }))), "Errors and limitations of the LLM"), mdx("p", null, "Although the LLM's responses were generally accurate I could notice quite a lot of limitations.\nFirstly, some of the responses contained generic statements that were not explicitly mentioned in the paper.\nSecondly, It had the tendency to repeat several of the answers, reused the same wording or repeated the previously stated facts across all the answers, This was a major downside that I felt. It suggests that the model was providing surface level reformulations instead of deeper, question specific insights.\nNext, Although the LLM captured the major results I felt it sometimes wasn't clear of the distinction between the metrics. I felt like it lacked precision when interpreting the experimental details, for example, It often stated that RAG provides the current up to date information which improves this pipeline.\nAccording to the paper RAG is applied in error cases to correct them by retrieving documents of old queries which can aid as feedback in correcting the current SQL queries. It has nothing to do with factual knowledge retrieval. I felt like it didnt give any details about the detailed workflow of the pipeline, like it didnt\ninclude any information about how the queries where classified based on the error types and what where the inputs given to the LLM model. It mainly only gave information of the 3 high level pipeline steps.\nI feel like it didnt analyse or read the different diagrams and the tables in the paper which actually had a lot of information regading the workflows and core mechanism of the pipeline which is very important to understand.\nIt also had images and tables that dealt with the finetuning and performance metrics, none of which it seemed to have reffered.\nThe model also missed out on a lot of small yet important details of the workflow, one such is that the ECPT\u2019s Treatment step retries up to three times until success or that the framework depends critically on having ground-truth SQL available, which limits deployment. These omissions show that the LLM can miss deeper implications unless\nexplicitly guided. Lastly, in the question about the limitations of this approach and if it would be useful, the model gave pretty generic responses such as the paper used limited baselines or that they needed to try on more datasets. These responses were not wrong but perhaps it could have also included limitations which are not generic and also had\nmore direct relevance to the paper itself. One of the limitation I noticed was that each experiment consumes arouns 2M prompt tokens and 100k completion tokens which is pretty large for a dataset of this size. Hence ECPT might be computationally expensive because each SQL correction involves multiple LLM calls and long prompts containing table descriptions,\nretrieved cases, and error information. This limitation was never mentioned by the LLM, when I would have expected it to notice such a thing."));
